Quantum-Inspired Search for Quantum Error Decoding: Constraint-Preserving Simulated Bifurcation Meets Local Degeneracy Aggregation

English manuscript, first draft — 29 October 2026; last revised 31 October 2026. Author information and acknowledgments will be supplied in the submission version. This draft uses the archived experiments and existing figures; it reports no new decoding experiments. Figure numbers below belong to this draft. Source and verification notes are provided separately in README_ZH.md.

Abstract

Quantum error decoding can be formulated as an Ising optimization problem by assigning lower energies to more probable error configurations. However, maximum-likelihood decoding must compare the total probabilities of logical classes of equivalent errors, rather than individual configurations. We bridge this gap by coupling quantum-inspired search with local degeneracy aggregation. Specifically, simulated bifurcation (SB) uses classical dynamics with synchronous variable updates to search for low-energy candidates in stabilizer-generator coordinates, preserving syndrome and logical class throughout the search. Around the lowest-energy candidate found in each logical class, local stabilizer-orbit aggregation sums a complete prescribed neighborhood in stabilizer-generator coordinates. An energy-change histogram with overlap corrections evaluates the radius-two local mass exactly, while this mass remains a truncated approximation to the full logical-class mass. On rotated surface codes under code-capacity depolarizing noise, the decoder with fixed search parameters and an aggregation radius selected on separate data outperforms matching and the tested correlated belief-propagation with ordered-statistics post-processing (BP+OSD) baseline in all 54 held-out conditions. For code distances up to nine, its logical error rate is at most 1.1513 times that of the exact or matrix-product-state reference. Exploratory increases in the SB step count improve larger-distance accuracy, while local aggregation improves on candidate-MAP inference at fixed candidate pools. At distance 13, selected simulated-bifurcation and simulated-annealing configurations attain similar error rates with different resource requirements: bifurcation uses two to four times more single-flip energy evaluations but 42–84 times fewer sequential update rounds in a model that parallelizes classes, restarts, and replicas or chains. These results demonstrate how quantum-inspired optimization can be combined with local probability aggregation for degenerate quantum decoding, while quantifying the tradeoff between decoding accuracy, total search work, and sequential update depth.

I. Introduction

Quantum error correction is a key ingredient of fault-tolerant quantum computation, protecting logical information from physical noise through redundant encoding and error recovery [1]. Recent experiments have demonstrated surface-code memories operating below threshold and key elements of integrated fault-tolerant processing architectures [2,3]. These advances bring the classical processing of error syndromes into focus: the protection achieved by an encoded system depends on the decoder used to infer suitable corrections. Recent improvements in decoding accuracy on experimental surface-code data illustrate the practical importance of this classical inference step [4]. Developing decoders that balance logical accuracy with computational cost is therefore an important part of making quantum error correction useful at larger scales.

The decoding objective is shaped by quantum degeneracy. Errors that differ by a stabilizer have the same action on the encoded information, so successful recovery need not identify the actual physical error. Instead, maximum-likelihood decoding selects the logical class with the largest total error probability [5]. This distinction matters when decoding is formulated as an Ising optimization problem: minimizing an energy associated with individual errors does not, by itself, determine the most probable logical class. Even a globally minimum-energy configuration can belong to a class whose total probability is smaller than that of another class. Connecting low-energy search to logical-class inference is the problem addressed here.

Several approaches address different parts of this problem. Minimum-weight perfect matching provides an efficient decoder for suitable error graphs [6]. Belief propagation with ordered-statistics post-processing extends decoding to more general parity-check structures [7]. Tensor-network methods approximate class probabilities by contracting a statistical model of the code [8,9]. Sampling approaches instead estimate these probabilities or their ratios, including Markov-chain Monte Carlo, population annealing, and worm methods [10,11,12]. These methods motivate a distinction between locating favorable configurations and estimating the total weight of a logical sector.

Ising formulations connect quantum decoding to physics-inspired optimization. Fujisaki et al. developed a Digital Annealer decoder based on a quadratic unconstrained binary optimization formulation and subsequently extended Ising-based decoding to circuit-level noise [13,14]. A complementary formulation assigns spins to stabilizer generators, making syndrome satisfaction exact by construction, as demonstrated for surface and color codes [15,16]. More recently, iterative low-order decoding has approximated cross-type error correlations through alternating subproblems to reduce the interaction order of joint Ising formulations [17]. These approaches establish an optimization interface, while leaving the choice of search dynamics and the treatment of logical-class probabilities as distinct design questions.

The quantum inspiration for simulated bifurcation (SB) comes from dynamical approaches to optimization. Quantum annealing seeks low-energy configurations by varying a Hamiltonian in time, typically reducing a transverse-field driver relative to the problem Hamiltonian [18]. Quantum bifurcation machines instead use networks of Kerr-nonlinear parametric oscillators, whose quantum adiabatic evolution through a bifurcation encodes candidate Ising solutions in oscillator states [19]. SB was developed from the corresponding classical nonlinear dynamics, replacing quantum-state evolution with numerical updates of continuous position and momentum variables [20]. It thus exemplifies physics-inspired computing: physical dynamical principles are used to design optimization algorithms that run on classical hardware.

SB’s synchronous updates support parallel GPU implementations [20,21,22] and scalable FPGA architectures [23,24,25], with favorable speed–quality tradeoffs reported on selected Ising benchmarks [21,22]. Subsequent developments include heating, bifurcation control, and tabu-based search [26,27,28], as well as direct treatment of higher-order objectives [29]. Recent implementations further extend dSB to discrete-phase variables in clock-dSB and native Potts variables in PdSB [30,31]. These advances broaden SB’s search capabilities, while degenerate quantum decoding additionally requires estimating logical-class probabilities.

Combining search with class-probability estimation is also an active direction. Truncated-free-energy decoding can reuse candidates produced by a stochastic inner decoder [32], while probabilistic formulations target class partition functions directly [33]. Our construction uses a different truncation: it sums a complete, prescribed neighborhood in generator space around one selected candidate per class. The sum includes configurations that the search did not visit. For radius two, locality permits this neighborhood to be counted through an energy-change histogram rather than explicitly enumerated.

We study the combination of constraint-preserving SB (CP-SB) and local stabilizer-orbit expansion (SOE), using the rotated surface code as a benchmark with an exact or tensor-network reference. Building on the established stabilizer-coordinate representation, our contributions are as follows:

- Complete local-neighborhood aggregation around constraint-preserving SB candidates. CP-SB searches within each syndrome-compatible logical class, and SOE sums the probabilities of all configurations in a complete, prescribed generator-space Hamming ball around the selected candidate. This includes configurations not visited by the search and defines a local truncation of the class probability mass.
- Exact radius-two local-mass evaluation using energy-change histograms and overlap corrections. We derive a counting formulation that combines additive energy changes for disjoint generator supports with exact corrections for overlapping pairs. Under bounded generator support and overlap degree, the local counting stage takes linear work once the physical center and support structure are available. We verify agreement with explicit neighborhood summation; exactness applies to the prescribed local sum, not the full logical-class probability.
- A controlled characterization of decoding accuracy and resource tradeoffs. We evaluate a decoder whose search configuration and aggregation rule are fixed before held-out testing (the fixed-configuration decoder) on a held-out grid and use paired exploratory comparisons to distinguish the effects of search-budget allocation and aggregation radius. Controlled comparisons between SB and simulated annealing with the same inference rule identify settings with no statistically significant accuracy difference in which SB uses more total flip-energy evaluations but fewer sequential update rounds. We report these work–depth tradeoffs separately from implementation-specific CPU timings.

The remainder of this paper is organized as follows. Section II presents the decoder construction, including stabilizer coordinates, SB dynamics, local aggregation, and the simulated-annealing control. Section III describes the evaluation protocol, baselines, statistical tests, and resource measures. Section IV reports decoding accuracy, finite-size and budget dependence, the effect of local aggregation, and computational costs. Section V discusses the interpretation, limitations, and possible extensions of the method. Section VI concludes. Appendix A gives the exact identities and locality conditions, Appendix B provides supplementary evidence and reproducibility information, and Appendix C reports structural circuit-level checks.

II. METHODS: Decoder construction

The decoder follows the search-then-aggregate workflow in Fig. 1. Starting from a measured syndrome, it constructs four logical classes of compatible errors and searches each class for a low-weight candidate. Each selected candidate then serves as the center of a complete local neighborhood, whose probability mass supplies the class score. Comparing these scores determines the correction. Search locates the centers; aggregation accounts for nearby stabilizer-equivalent errors, including configurations absent from the search output.

The construction proceeds from the decoding objective and feasible energy representation (Secs. II A–B) to SB candidate generation (Sec. II C) and local aggregation (Sec. II D). Section II E shows how to evaluate the radius-two sum exactly without enumerating every generator pair. Section II F introduces the simulated-annealing control used to distinguish search effects from aggregation effects.

[Figure 1: Constraint-preserving search and local degeneracy aggregation]

Figure 1. Constraint-preserving search and local degeneracy aggregation. For a measured syndrome s, a feasible seed is used to construct four syndrome-compatible logical classes L = 0, 1, 2, 3. Within each class, constraint-preserving simulated bifurcation (CP-SB) generates a candidate pool; the lowest Pauli-weight candidate in that pool becomes the center u_(L)^(*). Stabilizer-orbit expansion (SOE) then sums the error weights w^(|e_(L)(u)|), where w = p/[3(1 − p)], over all generator-coordinate configurations satisfying d_(H)(u, u_(L)^(*)) ≤ 2. The decoder selects the class with the largest resulting local mass Ẑ_(L)⁽²⁾ and returns its center error as the correction. The local sum is exact over the specified neighborhood, but does not equal the full logical-class probability. The diagram and bar lengths are schematic; the depicted neighborhood is in generator coordinates, not physical space.

A. Decoding objective and logical classes

We first specify what the decoder must infer: the logical class with the greatest total probability. This objective will guide both the search representation and the local approximation used to compare classes.

We consider independent depolarizing noise on a rotated planar surface code with distance d, n = d² data qubits, and m = n − 1 independent stabilizer generators. Each qubit experiences X, Y, or Z with probability p/3. For a binary symplectic error vector e ∈ 𝔽₂^(2n), let |e| count its nonidentity Pauli factors. Its probability is

$$
P(e)=(1-p)^n w^{|e|},\qquad w=\frac{p}{3(1-p)}.
\tag{1}
$$

The code encodes one logical qubit, giving four logical Pauli classes. We use the convention e = (e^(x), e^(z)), with Y represented by (1, 1) at a qubit but contributing only one unit to |e|. The measured syndrome is denoted by s.

Choose a syndrome-compatible seed e₀. Let G contain the independent stabilizer generators as rows, and let ℓ_(L) denote representatives of the four logical Pauli classes, L = 0, 1, 2, 3. Here G ∈ 𝔽₂^(m × 2n), all products and sums in Eq. (2) are over 𝔽₂, and the representatives ℓ_(L) are zero-syndrome logical operators. Every error in class L has a unique representation

e_(L)(u) = e₀ ⊕ ℓ_(L) ⊕ uG,   u ∈ 𝔽₂^(m).

The unnormalized class probability is

$$
Z_L(s)=\sum_u w^{|e_L(u)|}
=\sum_u e^{-\beta E_L(u)},\quad
E_L(u)=|e_L(u)|,\quad \beta=\log\frac{3(1-p)}p.
\tag{3}
$$

The conditional class probability is Z_(L)/∑_(L^(′))Z_(L^(′)). Maximum-likelihood decoding chooses the largest Z_(L), equivalently the smallest dimensionless free energy −β⁻¹log Z_(L) in the noise range studied. Here β is a statistical parameter fixed by the noise model, not a physical device temperature.

By contrast, a minimum-weight decision compares the smallest energies found in the classes. We call this the candidate-MAP decision: unless the search is exact, it need not equal the true minimum-weight decoder. We therefore separate the construction into two tasks: search for a low-energy center in each class, then estimate its class mass by summing a prescribed neighborhood.

B. Exact constraints and the Pauli-weight energy

To search for likely errors without leaving a prescribed class, we build the syndrome and logical constraints into the coordinates. The remaining task is to express the physical error weight as an energy of the generator spins.

The seed is obtained by matching the X and Z components separately. A binary linear-system solution would also provide a feasible seed, although its quality can affect a finite-budget search. Equation (2) guarantees that each binary configuration preserves both syndrome and logical class.

With generator spins σ_(j) = (−1)^(u_(j)), define

τ_(q)^(x) = (−1)^((e₀ ⊕ ℓ_(L))_(q)^(x))∏_(j : G_(j, q) = 1)σ_(j),   τ_(q)^(z) = (−1)^((e₀ ⊕ ℓ_(L))_(q)^(z))∏_(j : G_(j, n + q) = 1)σ_(j).

The exact nonidentity indicator is

$$
\mathbf1[e_q\ne I]
=\frac34-\frac14\tau_q^x-\frac14\tau_q^z
-\frac14\tau_q^x\tau_q^z.
\tag{4}
$$

Summing Eq. (4) gives the Pauli-weight Hamiltonian. On this surface code each qubit touches at most two generators of each type, so the multilinear representation contains terms of order at most four. No syndrome penalty or auxiliary spin is required. The continuous SB variables are an optimization device; the associated sign configurations are the feasible physical errors. We use E_(L)(σ) for the multilinear spin representation, which agrees with E_(L)(u) at σ_(j) = (−1)^(u_(j)).

C. Constraint-preserving SB candidate search

With a feasible energy representation in place, we use SB to produce candidate centers for aggregation. The dynamics below determines how candidates are generated; their energies alone do not determine the final class decision.

For one replica, x, y ∈ ℝ^(m) denote positions and momenta. We set sgn (0) = +1 and recover generator bits through u_(j) = (1 − sgn x_(j))/2. For each class and restart, CP-SB evolves R replicas for S steps:

y ← y + Δt[−(1 − a_(k))x + cf(sgn x)],   x ← x + Δt y,   a_(k) = (k + 1)/S.

The step index is k = 0, …, S − 1: the momentum update uses the current positions, and the position update uses the newly updated momenta. After each update, a coordinate outside [−1, 1] is set to its boundary sign and its momentum is reset to zero. The initial momenta vanish. The force is the negative gradient of the multilinear energy evaluated at a sign vertex:

$$
f_j(\sigma)=\frac{\sigma_j}{2}
\left[E_L(\sigma^{(j)})-E_L(\sigma)\right],
\tag{6}
$$

where σ^((j)) flips spin j. This identity permits evaluation from physical generator flips without introducing a quadratic surrogate. The coupling is c = κ/max (1, max_(j)B_(j)), where B_(j) is the sum of absolute coefficients of energy monomials containing j.

Identical monomials are combined before this coefficient bound is evaluated.

Each class receives a warm restart with initial positions 0.08 + 𝒩(0, 0.01²) and a random restart with positions uniform in (−0.1, 0.1). Each restart has R replicas. Terminal sign configurations and the initial binary seed u = 0 form the candidate pool. Duplicate bitstrings are removed and the pool is sorted by energy, with lexicographic bitstring order breaking energy ties. The first candidate becomes the aggregation center. Intermediate trajectory states are not added to this pool. Our budget notation R × S therefore corresponds to 8R trajectories across four classes and two restart modes. The fixed search configuration is R = 8, S = 100, Δt = 0.45, and κ = 0.70.

D. Complete local-neighborhood aggregation

The selected centers provide a starting point for comparing classes, but each center represents only one error configuration. We therefore add the probability weights of a prescribed set of stabilizer-equivalent neighbors before making the decision.

The aggregation set is a complete, prescribed Hamming ball in generator coordinates, including configurations that the search did not visit. Its radius measures generator flips, not physical-error weight or spatial extent. For the selected center u_(L)^(*), define

Ẑ_(L)^((r)) = ∑_(d_(H)(u, u_(L)^(*)) ≤ r)w^(E_(L)(u)),   L̂ = arg max_(L)Ẑ_(L)^((r)).

The decoder returns e₀ ⊕ ℓ_(L̂) ⊕ u_(L̂)^(*)G as a correction. If several computed scores agree with the maximum within relative tolerance 10⁻¹⁵ (zero absolute tolerance), the implementation chooses the lowest class index. Independent generator rows ensure that every term represents a distinct physical error. Thus Eq. (7) is an exact sum over the specified ball and a lower bound on the full unnormalized class mass. It is not an exact class probability or a certificate for the winning class. Increasing the radius increases each mass for fixed centers, but does not guarantee a more accurate ordering of classes.

The radius-one and radius-two balls contain 1 + m and $1+m+\binom m2$ configurations. At d = 13, these counts are 169 and 14,197 per class.

E. Exact radius-two local-mass evaluation

The radius-two neighborhood contains quadratically many generator pairs, making explicit summation costly. We exploit additivity for disjoint supports and correct the overlapping pairs to compute the same local sum with less work.

Let Δ_(j) be the energy change from flipping generator j. For generators with disjoint physical supports,

Δ_(jk) = Δ_(j) + Δ_(k).

We first count pair sums using a convolution of the single-flip histogram, remove self-pairs, and divide the ordered-pair counts by two. We then replace the additive contribution of each overlapping pair by its true energy change. Let N_(t) count all radius-two configurations with energy change t, including the center, single flips, and corrected pairs. The resulting local mass is

Ẑ_(L)⁽²⁾ = ∑_(t)N_(t)w^(E_(L)(u_(L)^(*)) + t).

The implementation constructs integer counts, checks their nonnegativity and total $1+m+\binom m2$, and sums the corresponding probabilities in ascending Pauli-weight order. The counts and the prescribed local sum are exact mathematically; floating-point powers and summation introduce rounding. Exactness does not extend to the omitted part of the logical class. Appendix A gives the full counting identity.

With bounded generator support and bounded overlap degree, the local counting stage costs O(m) once the physical center and support structure are available. This complexity concerns the local sum. The archived implementation also reconstructs physical candidates with dense matrix operations and builds a cached overlap structure; its complete preprocessing and candidate-selection pipeline is not covered by that linear bound.

F. Matched simulated-annealing control

To assess what SB contributes to the decoder, we replace only the search procedure by simulated annealing while retaining the feasible representation and aggregation rule. This control separates the choice of search dynamics from the local probability estimator.

The CP-SA control shares the feasible seeds, class Hamiltonians, candidate-pool construction, and aggregation rule. It uses single-generator Metropolis proposals with a geometric temperature schedule from 2.0 to 0.05 over W sweeps, with C chains in each restart mode. Its budget is denoted C × W. Warm chains start at u = 0, while random chains start from independent uniform binary states. Each sweep visits a fresh random permutation of all m generators in each chain. Non-increasing proposals are accepted; an increase ΔE > 0 is accepted with probability e^(−ΔE/T). For W ≥ 2, the geometric schedule includes both endpoints; a single sweep uses the initial temperature. This search temperature is distinct from the channel parameter β in Eq. (3). Although the SA solver also records best-ever states, the benchmark prediction pool uses only terminal states and the seed, with the same deduplication and tie rules as SB. A sweep contains m sequential proposals per chain and is not equivalent in work to one SB step. Holding the inference radius fixed allows the two search procedures to be compared without changing the probability estimator.

III. Evaluation design

The evaluation separates the performance of a fixed-configuration decoder from exploratory changes to its computational budget. We first select the aggregation radius on separate data and test the resulting decoder on a held-out grid. Budget comparisons then examine how search effort and aggregation affect accuracy. The following subsections specify the data separation, the roles of the baselines and reference, the statistical comparisons, and the distinction between search resources and implementation timings.

A. Evaluation stages and data separation

To distinguish parameter selection from performance assessment, Table 1 separates the statistical populations used in this study. A shot is one sampled physical Pauli error; decoder outcomes on the same shot are paired. LER denotes failure probability per code-capacity shot, not per syndrome-measurement round.

Table 1. Evaluation stages.

  -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Stage                        Conditions and samples                                                                                                        Role
  ---------------------------- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- ------------------------------------------------------------
  Selection                    d = 5, 7, 9, p = 0.10, 0.12; respectively 3,000, 2,000, and 1,000 shots per cell; 12,000 total                                Select SOE radius with the search parameters fixed

  Main evaluation grid         Six distances 3, 5, 7, 9, 11, 13, nine noise rates 0.04, 0.05, 0.06, 0.08, 0.10, 0.12, 0.14, 0.16, 0.18; 7.12 million shots   Held-out evaluation of CP-SB 8 × 100, radius two

  Budget scan                  First 10,000 shots per cell at d = 9, 11, 13, p = 0.08–0.18; incomplete configuration coverage at 0.18                        Exploratory allocation of replicas/chains and steps/sweeps

  Extended-budget evaluation   CP-SB 8 × 400 at d = 11, 13; full cells for p ≥ 0.08, first 100,000 shots for p ≤ 0.06                                        Evaluate a rule chosen after examining the budget scan
  -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Before the held-out grid was generated, radius two was selected only if it reduced pooled selection-set failures and passed an exact paired test at 0.05. It corrected 70 shots missed by radius one and missed 40 corrected by radius one, giving $P_{\rm test}=0.0054$. The resulting main decoder is CP-SB 8 × 100 with SOE radius two. Selection and held-out noise seeds are disjoint. Grid cell sizes range from 10,000 to one million shots.

The budget scan includes SB configurations 8 × 100, 16 × 100, 32 × 100, 64 × 100, 8 × 200, 8 × 400, and 32 × 400, and SA configurations 8 × 25, 8 × 50, 8 × 100, 8 × 200, 16 × 100, and 32 × 200. Each is evaluated with candidate-MAP and SOE radii one and two where available. Five configurations were run at p = 0.18. The extended-budget rule was chosen using these data; it is not an independent held-out confirmation.

B. Baselines and reference validation

The comparisons serve three roles: matching and BP+OSD provide practical accuracy baselines, the CP-SA control of Sec. II F, sharing the feasible representation and inference rule, isolates the search procedure, and REF supplies a high-accuracy estimate of logical-class decisions.

The matching baseline decodes the two Pauli components separately. Unit-weight matching and the tested probability-weighted PyMatching implementation produce identical grid predictions. The main BP baseline is correlated BP+OSD-CS with order seven, min-sum updates, and 3n iterations. Here H_(X) and H_(Z) are the binary X- and Z-stabilizer support matrices; H_(Z) detects the X component and H_(X) detects the Z component. Its binary check matrix is

$$
\begin{pmatrix}H_Z&0&H_Z\\0&H_X&H_X\end{pmatrix},
\tag{9}
$$

with X, Z, Y component priors p/3. The three binary blocks (x, z, y) map to physical components e^(x) = x ⊕ y and e^(z) = z ⊕ y. The implementation checks syndrome compatibility but imposes no explicit per-qubit exclusivity constraint on the three binary events. This construction is the specific correlated baseline tested here; it is not a claim about all BP-based decoders. It improves on the separately decoded BP+OSD baseline in all grid cells.

REF is exact class enumeration at d = 3. For d = 5, 7, 9, it uses an MPS decoder with bond dimension χ = 32. At d = 11, 13, it uses χ = 16, with χ = 32 replacements on selected difficult shots, capped at 3,000 per cell. Difficult shots were identified by a tested decoder failing or disagreeing with the χ = 16 prediction. When the difficult set exceeds the cap, 3,000 shots are selected by sampling without replacement with a fixed random state. This targeted check uses simulation information and is a reference-validation procedure, not a deployable decoder-selection rule.

On the checked difficult subsets at d ≥ 11, the χ = 16/32 comparison has at most two disagreements per cell, and the χ = 32/64 comparison has at most one. The latter check covers 18 cells with 401–3,000 shots each. These observations support a stable numerical reference on the tested subsets; they do not certify exact ML for every larger-code shot. Ratios below one on a finite sample need not imply a decoder with lower expected error than exact ML.

C. Statistical comparisons

Shared physical-error samples allow differences between decoders to be assessed shot by shot. We report uncertainty in individual error rates separately from paired effect sizes and multiple-comparison tests.

Individual LER intervals are Wilson 95% intervals. Paired differences use the observed discordant outcomes, with exact two-sided McNemar tests and the archived Holm corrections across cells within each comparison family. The paired intervals in Fig. S2 are conservative difference intervals: Clopper–Pearson bounds are constructed for the two discordant-event probabilities with Bonferroni allocation, and then subtracted. They have at least 95% coverage under the binomial shot model, but are not simultaneous Holm-adjusted intervals. Filled markers and interval crossings therefore encode different statistical procedures. Pooled discordant counts across heterogeneous cells are descriptive summaries.

For each fixed decoder pair on the main evaluation grid, Holm correction covers all 54 cells; the different implementations of REF across distances remain within the same reference-comparison family. The correlated-BP comparisons likewise use 54-cell families. In the configuration-pair budget comparisons, each configuration pair and inference rule defines a separate family over the available cells, without imputing missing configurations. The three radius-two comparisons displayed in Fig. S2 contain 15, 18, and 18 cells, respectively. Extended-budget comparisons use separate 18-cell families for each specified decoder pair. These corrections control multiplicity within the stated families, not across all exploratory choices in the study.

The difference intervals are computed by assigning a failure probability of 0.025 to each of the two Clopper–Pearson intervals, then differencing their endpoints in the LER convention. Thus the interval plotted for methods A and B estimates LER(A) minus LER(B), with negative values favoring A.

D. Computational resources and timing

Accuracy alone does not distinguish a search that performs more work in parallel from one that performs less work sequentially. We therefore report total flip-energy evaluations and update depth separately, and use CPU timings to characterize the particular implementations.

We distinguish two search resources:

$$
N_{\Delta E}^{\rm SB}=8RSm,\quad
N_{\Delta E}^{\rm SA}=8CWm;\qquad
D_{\rm SB}=S,\quad D_{\rm SA}=Wm.
\tag{10}
$$

The first counts single-generator flip-energy evaluations. The second counts update rounds when logical classes, restarts, and replicas or chains run in parallel, while SA updates within a chain remain sequential. These quantities exclude setup, inference, communication, and the internal cost of an energy evaluation. They are neither end-to-end operation counts nor measured hardware latency. Alternative parallel SA implementations are outside this comparison.

Archived wall times are medians over 100 unique nontrivial syndromes at p = 0.10 on an AMD EPYC 9754 node using a single-process Python 3.11/NumPy 2.4 implementation. SB batches variable updates; SA performs individual proposals in Python. Matching and BP+OSD use compiled components. The timing scripts default the OpenMP, OpenBLAS, MKL, and NumExpr thread counts to one when these variables are unset; they do not override existing environment settings. The archived environment summary does not record these thread variables, so single-process execution alone does not independently verify single-thread execution. Timing therefore complements, rather than replaces, the resource counts.

Figure 4 retains the historical dense-search and explicit-SOE timings from the original preview PDF. Table 2 separately reports the later sparse-search and histogram-SOE implementation, together with the explicit-SOE comparison and archived baseline timings. Both campaigns record 100 unique nontrivial syndromes per reported condition at p=0.10; the optimized campaign reuses the historical syndromes and solver seeds. Per-code support structures are prepared once and excluded from the optimized per-syndrome timings. Search-only time excludes seed construction and aggregation; end-to-end CP time includes the seed, search, and the selected SOE radius. The raw-log label “total” can include multiple inference evaluations and is not used as a substitute for these stage definitions. MPS timings refer to the stated fixed bond dimension, not the complete targeted-validation REF procedure.

IV. Results

We first establish the held-out accuracy of the fixed-configuration decoder, then examine two ways to improve its decisions: allocating more search effort and aggregating probability around fixed candidates. The final comparison separates total search work, sequential update depth, and measured implementation costs. Figures 2–5 show the main accuracy and timing results; Figs. S1–S2 provide resource-normalized and paired statistical comparisons.

A. Accuracy of the fixed-configuration decoder

The first question is whether the fixed selection rule transfers to held-out errors, and how its performance changes with code distance.

The main decoder improves on matching and the tested correlated BP+OSD baseline in every held-out cell. All 54 comparisons against each baseline remain significant after Holm correction; the largest adjusted values are 8.7 × 10⁻¹² and 0.0077, respectively. Against matching, the decoder corrects 55,081 shots that matching misses, versus 17,007 in the opposite direction. Against correlated BP+OSD the corresponding totals are 41,739 and 21,099.

The comparison with REF reveals a size dependence. For d ≤ 9, the main decoder’s LER is at most 1.1513 times REF (approximately 1.15). Of these 36 cells, 27 do not show a significant paired difference from REF under the Holm comparison families defined in Sec. III C. This lack of detection does not establish equivalence. At d = 11 and 13, the maximum ratios over the tested noise rates are approximately 1.33 and 1.87, respectively. Thus the same fixed parameters that perform well on smaller codes leave a substantial larger-code gap.

[Figure 2]

Figure 2. Accuracy relative to REF. Each panel shows one code distance, with physical error probability on the horizontal axis and LER/REF on the vertical axis. The horizontal reference corresponds to a ratio of one. Solid main-method curves use the fixed-configuration decoder. Purple downward-triangle extended-budget curves at distances 11 and 13 are exploratory and use their own evaluated subsets, including only 100,000 shots per low-noise cell. Ratios are point estimates; no paired ratio intervals are implied. The low-noise larger-code reference has few failures, making those ratios especially sensitive to sampling. The CP-SA curve uses radius one, whereas the main CP-SB curve uses radius two; their separation does not isolate the search dynamics.

B. Finite-size behavior and search-budget allocation

The larger-code gap motivates testing whether additional search effort can recover accuracy while retaining the aggregation rule. In the tested extended-budget conditions, increasing the SB step count reduces the LER point estimates. In the extended-budget evaluation, raising S from 100 to 400 lowers the point-estimated LER in all 18 cells at d = 11, 13; 12 pass the Holm criterion defined in Sec. III C. For p ≥ 0.08, the ratio to REF changes from 1.20–1.33 to 1.09–1.12 at d = 11, and from 1.38–1.87 to 1.07–1.23 at d = 13.

A comparison at equal flip-energy evaluation counts examines budget allocation within the tested range; equality of this count does not imply equal elapsed time or end-to-end work. CP-SB 8 × 400 and 32 × 100 perform the same number of flip-energy evaluations. At d = 13, that number is 4,300,800. At p = 0.12, their radius-two ratios on the 10,000-shot budget subset are approximately 1.17 and 1.28. Across the 15 matched cells, the longer SB run wins 1,935 discordant shots and loses 1,643. Its LER is lower in nine cells, but only one cell passes Holm correction. The evidence favors longer SB runs particularly in the difficult larger-code region; it does not support a universal per-cell advantage.

The larger budget also changes the finite-size crossing of the d = 11 and 13 curves, from approximately p = 0.1167 to 0.1581, compared with approximately 0.1765 for REF. These point estimates use linear interpolation of the difference in log-LER between adjacent noise rates: 0.10–0.12, 0.14–0.16, and 0.16–0.18, respectively. No crossing confidence intervals are estimated. This is evidence that the observed crossing depends on the search budget. It is not an estimate of the asymptotic threshold: the sizes are limited and the enlarged-budget rule was selected after inspecting the scan.

[Figure 3]

Figure 3. Finite-size behavior under fixed and enlarged search budgets. Physical error probability labels the curves; the horizontal axis is code distance and the vertical axis is logical error rate. Panels compare the fixed-configuration decoder, the decoder with 400 steps at distances 11 and 13, and REF. The change of curve ordering illustrates finite-budget sensitivity, rather than an established asymptotic threshold. The reported crossing is where the distance-11 and distance-13 LERs become equal as functions of physical error probability; it is not a crossing between the colored curves in this distance-axis view.

[Figure 4]

Figure 4. Historical CPU accuracy–time benchmark at distance 13. Columns show physical error probabilities 0.12 and 0.16. Top panels plot LER/REF with radius-one aggregation against search time; bottom panels use end-to-end time and connect radius-one and radius-two results. Blue denotes SB and orange SA; the star and diamond denote the plotted MPS and correlated BP+OSD comparators. Times are from the earlier implementation recorded in the preview PDF, measured at physical error probability 0.10. They are not the optimized implementation timings in Table 2. The MPS marker represents the plotted reference comparator, not a timing measurement of the full adaptive REF pipeline.

Figure 4 documents the earlier implementation’s accuracy–time tradeoff, including the substantial cost of explicit radius-two aggregation. Table 2 separately reports the later histogram implementation. The upper panels illustrate historical search-budget tradeoffs; the lower panels motivate the aggregation-cost comparison in Sec. IV D. This figure is not a performance summary of the optimized implementation; the paired budget evidence and implementation-independent resource plots are retained in Figs. S1–S2.

C. What local aggregation adds after search

Search quality alone does not determine decoding accuracy. To isolate the inference rule, we retain each search configuration’s candidate pool and compare candidate-MAP with local aggregation. On the 10,000-shot budget subset at d = 13, p = 0.12, CP-SB 32 × 400 has candidate-MAP LER/REF of 1.382, which becomes 1.115 for radius one and 1.096 for radius two. CP-SA 32 × 200 gives 1.376, 1.099, and 1.093, respectively. These comparisons isolate the effect of replacing a minimum-candidate-energy decision by local mass aggregation within each search configuration. They do not require assuming that either search has found the global minimum. These numerical examples use larger budgets than Fig. 5, which displays the corresponding inference comparisons for the fixed-budget and 8×400 searches.

Radius two provides a smaller additional benefit over radius one. On the main evaluation grid, its LER is lower in 50 of 54 cells, with three individually significant after correction. The pooled discordant counts are 5,222 in favor and 4,337 against. The pre-grid selection result established the main radius; the full-grid comparison describes its subsequent behavior.

[Figure 5]

Figure 5. Candidate-MAP and local aggregation. The six-panel figure compares candidate-MAP, SOE radius one, and SOE radius two against REF as a function of physical error probability. Solid curves use the fixed-budget search, and dashed open-marker curves use the extended budget where available. Comparisons at fixed search isolate a change of inference rule. Vertical gaps are changes in decoding performance, not an additive causal decomposition of error sources. In particular, the residual radius-two gap cannot be assigned entirely to truncation without independently controlling search quality.

At the largest scanned budgets, CP-SB 32 × 400 and CP-SA 32 × 200 retain a radius-two ratio of approximately 1.075–1.129 at d = 13, p ≥ 0.10. No cell in the right panel of Fig. S2 passes the Holm significance criterion for the difference between them. A similar residual gap from two search dynamics is consistent with limitations of the single-center local estimator. However, these data do not establish identical shot-by-shot decisions, global search convergence, or statistical equivalence. Missed probability outside the chosen ball is a plausible contributor; unobserved improvements from a stronger search remain possible.

D. Work, sequential rounds, and elapsed time

We next ask what computational costs accompany the observed accuracy. Figures S1–S2 show a work–depth tradeoff for the tested SB/SA configurations, rather than a uniformly better searcher. At d = 13, SB 8 × 400 uses four times the flip-energy evaluations of SA 8 × 100, but 400 update rounds instead of 16,800. SB 32 × 400 uses twice the evaluations of SA 32 × 200, but 400 rounds instead of 33,600. Their radius-two LER differences are not significant in any of the 18 cells for either pair under the Holm families defined in Sec. III C. The point estimates are close, while the operation counts differ by factors of two to four in work and 42–84 in rounds.

