The histogram implementation makes local aggregation a small part of the measured decoder time. For the SB 8×100 candidate pools at distance 13, it reduces radius-two evaluation time from 3.52781 s to 0.00081604 s, approximately 4,300-fold. This is a stage-specific improvement, not a decoder-wide speedup. A recorded consistency check over 42,800 decodes found unchanged decisions, maximum relative class-score differences of 1.2 × 10⁻¹⁵, and identical candidate pools between sparse and full-vector search evaluations. The check includes radii one and two on the same 42,800 decodes and records no decision disagreements, including no tie-related disagreements. These are finite implementation checks, not a guarantee of bit-identical scores or new accuracy measurements.

MPS remains an important comparator: the extended CP-SB pipeline takes 0.375 s at distance 13, while χ = 16 MPS takes 0.540 s in the archived timing benchmark. The CP-SB decoder still has a measurable accuracy gap, and the validated REF includes targeted larger-bond calculations beyond χ = 16. The timing table therefore does not demonstrate dominance over the reference pipeline. Likewise, the SB/SA CPU ratio mixes algorithmic structure with batching and language overhead. A parallel implementation must measure memory traffic, synchronization, and finite hardware occupancy before the update-round advantage can be interpreted as a latency advantage.

V. Discussion

The benchmark shows how low-energy search and local probability aggregation can work together in degenerate quantum decoding. The coordinate representation preserves both syndrome and logical class, and the radius-two algorithm evaluates the probability weight of the complete prescribed generator-space neighborhood exactly. This local mass remains an approximation to the full logical-class mass: different classes can have different fractions of probability outside their neighborhoods, so exact local evaluation does not certify their ordering. On the tested surface codes under independent depolarizing noise, aggregation improves on candidate-MAP inference at fixed search configurations (Sec. IV C). The main decoder has LER at most 1.1513 times REF for distances up to nine, while larger distances benefit from additional search effort in the exploratory extended-budget evaluation (Secs. IV A–B).

The remaining accuracy gap motivates examining both search and the mass estimator. At matched flip-energy evaluation counts, longer SB runs yield lower LER point estimates in nine of fifteen tested cells, with one difference remaining significant after Holm correction. This comparison motivates examining run length alongside replica count, rather than prescribing a universally preferred allocation. One hypothesis is that the shorter ramp limits progress within each trajectory; trajectory-level diagnostics would be needed to distinguish this explanation from initialization and other schedule effects. The similar residual gaps at the largest scanned SB and SA budgets also motivate broader mass estimators, but do not establish search convergence or isolate truncation as the sole source of error. Further comparisons should vary the estimator at fixed candidate pools and vary search at a fixed estimator. Multiple-center or adaptive neighborhoods are possible extensions; overlapping configurations must be counted only once, and the resulting accuracy gains and deduplication costs would need to be measured.

These accuracy questions are coupled to the cost of computing class scores. In the tested implementation, the histogram formulation makes exact radius-two aggregation a small part of measured decoder time while preserving the prescribed local sum (Sec. IV D). Its stage-specific speedup does not represent a decoder-wide speedup, and transferring its cost advantage requires checking the support and overlap assumptions in Sec. II E. SB offers a separate resource tradeoff: in the selected SB–SA comparisons, it uses more flip-energy evaluations but fewer sequential update rounds. The tensor-network reference separately benchmarks accuracy and archived implementation time; it is not compared using the same operation-count model. Establishing a hardware benefit requires matched implementations and measurements of end-to-end latency, throughput, memory traffic, and synchronization costs. The historical timings in Fig. 4 and optimized timings in Table 2 describe different implementation versions, and neither establishes dominance over the full adaptive REF pipeline.

With these computational conditions in view, the algebra extends beyond surface codes. For a binary stabilizer code encoding k qubits, Eq. (2) holds with an independent stabilizer basis and 4^(k) logical Pauli classes. Codes with small fixed k and a suitable independent local basis, such as appropriate planar color-code families, are natural next tests; stabilizer-coordinate optimization has already been applied to color codes [16]. Efficient radius-two evaluation additionally requires checking bounded generator supports and overlap structure, and the present surface code’s four-body energy representation is not a general property of stabilizer codes. The mass captured by a fixed-radius ball depends on the generator basis, while dependent generators can count the same physical error repeatedly. For higher-rate qLDPC codes, enumerating all logical classes becomes a separate obstacle even when supports remain sparse. Thus the transferable coordinate construction supplies a starting point, rather than an established efficient general qLDPC decoder or a demonstrated advantage on codes less favorable to tensor contraction.

Circuit-level decoding requires a further change from data-qubit errors to fault mechanisms. Within a fixed detector record and hypothesized logical-observable record, movements in the kernel of the stacked detector and logical-observable matrices preserve both records. Using the bivariate-bicycle circuit construction associated with Ref. [34] and a detector-error representation implemented with Stim [35], our archived checks examine such coordinates for surface-code memory circuits and a bivariate-bicycle example (Appendix C). Most generators selected from the heuristic candidate construction are low weight, but completing the basis also adds global null-space elements; low generator weight alone does not bound the number of generators touching each fault mechanism. These checks motivate a fault-space decoder, but do not validate a complete search-and-aggregation pipeline. Although mechanism likelihood weights are already represented in the code, a decoder must still integrate the appropriate fault likelihoods, account for global basis elements, and handle nonuniform weights in local aggregation. Circuit-level accuracy and computational performance remain to be measured. These structural observations are retained in archived reports; the underlying circuit and matrix artifacts are absent from the current project snapshot (Appendix C).

VI. Conclusion

We have combined simulated-bifurcation search with probability aggregation over complete prescribed stabilizer-coordinate neighborhoods to connect low-energy optimization to logical-class inference. The coordinates preserve syndrome and logical class without penalty terms, while a support-based histogram evaluates the radius-two mass exactly without enumerating all generator pairs. The resulting local mass remains an approximation to the full logical-class mass. On held-out rotated-surface-code data under independent depolarizing noise, the main decoder improves over the tested matching and correlated BP+OSD baselines, while exploratory increases in the SB step count improve larger-distance accuracy. At fixed candidate pools, local aggregation improves on candidate-MAP inference. In the selected SB–SA comparisons, SB uses more flip-energy evaluations but fewer sequential update rounds; the corresponding hardware latency remains to be measured. These results support a search-then-aggregate approach to code-capacity decoding, with further progress depending on the joint design of search budgets, local mass estimators, and their computational implementation.

Appendix A. Exact identities and locality conditions

A.1. Constraint preservation and counting

All binary operations are over 𝔽₂, and Pauli errors are identified up to overall phase. Let H be the syndrome map in the (X ∣ Z) convention, including the symplectic pairing, so that HG^(T) = 0. When the measured checks are the rows of G in the same order, H = GJ, where

$$
J=\begin{pmatrix}0&I_n\\I_n&0\end{pmatrix}.
$$

A different ordering of measured checks gives the corresponding row permutation of this map. Logical representatives have zero syndrome, so Eq. (2) gives

He_(L)(u)^(T) = He₀^(T) + Hℓ_(L)^(T) + HG^(T)u^(T) = s.

Varying u multiplies the error by a stabilizer and therefore preserves its logical class. Since rank_(𝔽₂)G = m, equality uG = vG implies u = v. A generator-space ball thus maps injectively to physical Pauli errors up to phase. Dependent generators would still preserve the constraints, but could count the same physical error more than once.

For 0 < p < 1, the weight w = p/[3(1 − p)] is positive and finite. For any nonnegative integer radius r, the ball ℬ_(r)(u_(L)^(*)) contains its center, and

Z_(L) − Ẑ_(L)^((r)) = ∑_(u ∉ ℬ_(r)(u_(L)^(*)))w^(E_(L)(u)) ≥ 0,   0 < Ẑ_(L)^((r)) ≤ Z_(L).

This subset-sum bound concerns unnormalized masses. It supplies no bound on the class-dependent fraction of omitted mass and does not certify the ordering of logical classes or calibrated conditional probabilities.

A.2. Flip energies and force

Any multilinear energy can be written as E(σ) = A_(j)(σ_( ≠ j)) + σ_(j)b_(j)(σ_( ≠ j)), where neither A_(j) nor b_(j) depends on σ_(j). Flipping spin j gives

Δ_(j) = E(σ^((j))) − E(σ) = −2σ_(j)b_(j).

Since σ_(j)² = 1 at a spin vertex,

$$
\frac{\sigma_j}{2}\Delta_j=-b_j
=-\frac{\partial E}{\partial\sigma_j},
$$

which proves Eq. (6). The function b_(j) here is distinct from the coefficient bound B_(j) used to normalize the coupling in Sec. II C. The SB update evaluates this identity at σ = sgn (x), with the stated zero-sign convention; it does not identify the interaction force with −∇E(x) at arbitrary continuous positions. This is an identity for the chosen energy, not a theorem of monotonic energy decrease or convergence of the SB updates.

A.3. Radius-two histogram

Fix a logical class and its center u_(L)^(*), and write E₀ = E_(L)(u_(L)^(*)). Let ϵ_(j) be the binary unit vector in generator coordinate j. All changes are measured from the same center:

Δ_(j) = E_(L)(u_(L)^(*) ⊕ ϵ_(j)) − E₀,   Δ_(jk) = E_(L)(u_(L)^(*) ⊕ ϵ_(j) ⊕ ϵ_(k)) − E₀  (j < k).

Write h_(t) = #{j : Δ_(j) = t} for integer t, and define (h * h)_(t) = ∑_(a ∈ ℤ)h_(a)h_(t − a). This convolution counts ordered generator pairs, including self-pairs. Removing the self-pairs and dividing by two gives the histogram of additive changes over all unordered distinct pairs:

$$
P_t=\frac12\left[(h*h)_t-\sum_j\mathbf1[t=2\Delta_j]\right].
\tag{A1}
$$

Because the Pauli-weight objective is a sum of single-qubit indicators, generators with disjoint physical-qubit supports satisfy Δ_(jk) = Δ_(j) + Δ_(k). Let 𝒪 contain each pair j < k whose physical-qubit supports intersect, including overlap between X and Z components on the same qubit. For each such pair, we remove its additive contribution and insert its true energy change. Including the center and all single flips, the exact radius-two histogram is

N_(t) = 1[t = 0] + h_(t) + P_(t) + ∑_((j, k) ∈ 𝒪)(1[t = Δ_(jk)] − 1[t = Δ_(j) + Δ_(k)]).

Thus N_(t) ≥ 0 and $\sum_tN_t=1+m+\binom m2$: each overlap correction preserves the total count. With independent generator rows, these counts enumerate distinct physical errors, and

Ẑ_(L)⁽²⁾ = ∑_(t)N_(t)w^(E₀ + t).

The histogram identities are exact in integer arithmetic; the implementation uses fixed-width integer counts and floating-point probability evaluation. Exact counting therefore assumes no integer overflow, while numerical scores remain subject to floating-point rounding and range limits. Exact evaluation of this local sum does not extend to the omitted part of the logical class.

If every generator touches at most a fixed K qubits, single-flip changes lie in [−K, K] and pair changes in [−2K, 2K]. If |𝒪| = O(m), the single-flip counts and overlap corrections require O(m) local work, with fixed-size convolution. Bounded overlap degree is sufficient for this condition. The bound assumes that the physical center and support incidence data have been supplied; it does not cover the complete candidate-selection or decoding pipeline. Dense construction of the overlap matrix, dense reconstruction of candidates, pool scoring, and cache storage are separate costs in the archived code. Even cached-structure lookup constructs a key from the dense generator matrix. The bound also relies on the integer Pauli-weight objective: arbitrary position-dependent likelihood weights require a fresh complexity analysis, and correlated objectives require rechecking disjoint-support additivity.

